ded_on_rejection": false, "error": null, "exec_mode": "local", "failure_class": "solved", "failure_reason": null, "faults_served": 0, "final_answer_complete": true, "final_answer_present": true, "final_answer_source": "json", "fresh_subagents": 0, "harness": "omp", "harness_version": "11.9.0 (/workspace/pi-check/cache2/bin/omp/ab1db22d1d3f60e8f443006a5aa3d5b4158aa3cf/omp)", "harness_version_source": "host", "host": "grok-bot-vm-412615941", "image_digest": null, "last_activity_age_s": null, "list_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "Root cause: `to_cents` truncates float error \u2014 `19.99 * 100 == 1998.9999\u2026` \u2192 `int()` gives `1998`. Same for 0.29 and 4.35.\nNo pytest available; run tests manually.\nShell quirk with the inline script; use a file.\nI fixed the bug. The cause was `ledger/currency.py::to_cents`, which did `int(amount * 100)`. Floats can't store most decimal prices exactly, so `19.99 * 100` comes out as `1998.999\u2026` and `int()` cuts it down to `1998`. Prices of 0.29 and 4.35 lose a cent the same way, and those are the inputs in all four tests.\n\n**Fix:** `to_cents` now turns the amount into a `Decimal` by way of `str()`, so 19.99 is read as written instead of as the float's binary value. It multiplies by 100 and rounds half-up to a whole cent. That matches the half-up rounding `discounts.py` and `tax.py` already use.\n\n**Verification:** pytest isn't installed here, so I called every test function in `tests/test_totals.py` and `tests/test_report.py` directly. All 5 passed. I also spot-checked these conversions:\n- `19.99 \u2192 1999`\n- `0.29 \u2192 29`\n- `4.35 \u2192 435`\n- `10 \u2192 1000`\n- `\"1.005\" \u2192 101`\n- `-2.675 \u2192 -268`\n\nThe last one shows that half-up rounds negative amounts away from zero.\n\n**Behavior change:** a price with more than two decimal places now rounds to the nearest cent instead o